WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.139
TOM: I am with Kate Oliver and Andy Collins

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:04.620
from the Centre for Functional Nanomaterials
at the University of Bristol.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.790
And they're going to show me how we see tiny
tiny tiny things.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.049
KATE: So we're nanotechnologists, and for
us, a tiny thing is an atom.

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:13.910
TOM: Right. Okay.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.860
KATE: So we have one way of seeing atoms.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.330
It's called a scanning tunnelling microscope.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.660
And it uses the fact that if you bring a tip
close to a surface,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.699
there's a probability that because of quantum
--

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:25.810
TOM: I love that explanation.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:29.320
KATE: -- the electrons will go from the
surface to the tip.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.070
So basically, as you move the tip along,

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.899
if you hit a bump, more electrons will tunnel
up into the tip.

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:36.660
And that will give you an idea of how close
the surface is.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.750
TOM: But that means you need a tip that is
one atom thick.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.800
KATE: It means you need a tip that is very
small.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.859
TOM: Okay. Which is where you come in.

00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:48.449
ANDY: So I'm going to show you how to make
a tip that's one atom thick.

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:52.039
You need to get it as sharp as you possibly
can for good imaging.

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:54.039
We've got a secret weapon of nanoscience:

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:55.579
Pliers. And wire cutters.

00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:57.180
TOM: I kind of assumed there'd be some kind of

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.519
hideously expensive equipment required in
this, but...

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:02.399
ANDY: So I've got some platinum wire.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:04.309
TOM: Okay, that is hideously expensive.

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:06.340
ANDY: It's a platinum iridium alloy.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.570
And we use platinum so that it doesn't conduct oxygen,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:10.990
which would make the tip fatter.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:15.040
Because we want one single, conductive atom
at the surface of the tip.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.030
So if I hold that wire in my pliers,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.650
and I apply the wire cutters at a 45 degree
angle...

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.450
..there we go!

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.540
That means you've got something that, in theory,
is atomically sharp.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.229
TOM: That's fantastic. So what do you do if
it's not conductive?

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:31.979
Because quantum tunnelling only works with
something that's conductive.

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:33.640
KATE: This is an atomic force microscope.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.229
TOM: It's really cool! And it's actually scanning
at the minute?

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:44.229
KATE: It is. You can see on the screen here,
that is -- 20 micrometres across.

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:48.090
So that is about a fifth of the width of one
of your hairs, Tom.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.450
But we wouldn't be able to see anything on here

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.290
if it wasn't against a really flat,
high-contrast background.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:59.680
Luckily, we have a number of minerals that
come in convenient flat sheets.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.619
One of these is mica, which I have here.

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:05.689
But we can't be sure that we've only got one
layer on the surface.

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:09.170
So for that, we have another
high-tech nanoscience tool.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:10.800
TOM: Which is sellotape.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.580
KATE: Yes.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.550
Adhere this down.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.530
And it's stuck to just one of these layers
of mica, or maybe a couple.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.360
TOM: But the top layers?

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.090
KATE: Yes.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.690
And then: peel it off, and can you see the
faint outline?

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.340
TOM: There is. There is a faint outline of
a square on there.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.810
Which is -- is that just a one atom thick
layer of mica?

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.090
KATE: Yep. And that implies that what's left
is also entirely flat.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.590
Giving us a really good contrast for any atoms,
molecules, species --

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.340
these are some fibres we've got on top of
this surface --

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.530
that we want to have a look at and see what
they can do for us that's really useful.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.530
TOM: So we have million-pound equipment 
around this lab.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:49.860
And then we've got pliers and sticky tape.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.430
ANDY: Yes.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.780
TOM: That is wonderful. Kate Oliver, Andy
Collins,

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.700
from the Centre for Functional Nanomaterials
at the University of Bristol.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.810
Thank you very much, guys!

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.780
